  { q: 'Is a raven visiting your house a bad omen?', a: 'In Western folk tradition, a raven landing on a roof, perching at a window, or repeatedly visiting a house was widely interpreted as a death omen — a warning that someone in the household would soon die. This tradition is documented across British, Irish, and continental European folklore and is reflected in Poe\'s "The Raven". However, the same behaviour in other cultural traditions (Norse, Pacific Northwest Indigenous) would be interpreted positively — as a sign of Odin\'s attention or Raven\'s companionship. Scientifically, a raven visiting a house likely reflects habituation to humans, opportunistic food-seeking, or territorial exploration.' },
  { q: 'Why would a raven come up to you?', a: 'Ravens approach humans for several practical reasons: they may associate humans with food (at parks, campgrounds, or waste sites where they have been fed); they may be young, recently-fledged birds less experienced with the appropriate flight distance from humans; or they may be unusually bold individuals. Wild ravens in areas with high human density (urban areas, national parks, tourist sites) often become remarkably habituated and will approach closely for food. A raven approaching you in a genuinely wild area is unusual and may indicate the bird is ill or habituated to humans through previous contact.' },
  { q: 'What does a raven symbolise spiritually?', a: 'In spiritual and metaphysical frameworks, the raven is frequently associated with: transformation and change (the raven as a herald of significant transitions); hidden knowledge and wisdom (the raven as a guide to unseen truths, connecting to Odin\'s ravens as instruments of omniscience); death and rebirth (the raven at the threshold between life and death, as in Celtic tradition); and trickery and creativity (the Trickster-Raven of Pacific Northwest and Siberian traditions who creates through deception and cunning). What a raven "means" spiritually to a specific person depends entirely on which tradition they engage with and their personal relationship with the bird.' },
];
